  "body": {
    "id": "lem:score-error-set-modulus",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Let \\(P\\in\\mathcal P_W\\), let \\(r\\geq0\\), and let \\(\\widetilde q:K\\to\\mathbb R\\) satisfy \\(\\sup_{\\theta\\in K}|\\widetilde q(\\theta)-q_P(\\theta)|\\leq r\\). For \\(A_r=\\{\\theta\\in K:\\widetilde q(\\theta)\\leq r\\}\\),\n\\[\n d_{\\to}\\{A_r,\\Theta_I(P)\\}\n \\leq \\frac{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)+2r}-\\sqrt{\\delta(P)}}{c_\\beta}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nConsequently, if \\(0\\leq\\delta(P)\\leq C_\\delta r\\), then \\(d_{\\to}\\{A_r,\\Theta_I(P)\\}\\leq \\sqrt{2r}/c_\\beta\\), while if \\(\\delta(P)\\geq\\delta_0\\), then \\(d_{\\to}\\{A_r,\\Theta_I(P)\\}\\leq r/(c_\\beta\\sqrt{\\delta_0})\\). Thus the same deterministic map has a square-root contact modulus and a linear fixed-interior modulus.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "K",
      "q",
      "d_arrow",
      "delta",
      "C_delta",
      "delta_0",
      "c_beta"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:identified-set-endpoint-domain",
      "def:watson-class",
      "def:watson-published-reduction",
      "def:contact-class",
      "def:interior-class",
      "ass:fixed-relevance",
      "ass:parameter-margin"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(b=\\|\\beta(P)\\|_2\\) and \\(c=\\check\\theta(P)\\).  Fixed relevance gives \\(b\\geq c_\\beta>0\\).  The orthogonal decomposition proved in the endpoint-domain lemma gives\n\\[\n q_P(\\theta)=b^2(\\theta-c)^2-\\delta(P),\n \\qquad\n \\Theta_I(P)=\\left[c-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)}}b,\n                         c+\\frac{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)}}b\\right], \\tag{1}\n\\]\nwith no \\(K\\)-truncation under the parameter-margin assumption.  Let\n\\[\n A_r=\\{\\theta\\in K:\\widetilde q(\\theta)\\leq r\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(A_r=\\varnothing\\), the convention defining \\(d_{\\to}\\) gives zero loss and there is nothing to prove.  Otherwise the explicit uniform error bound forces \\(r\\geq0\\), and every \\(\\theta\\in A_r\\) obeys\n\\[\n q_P(\\theta)\\leq\\widetilde q(\\theta)+r\\leq2r.\n\\]\nUsing (1),\n\\[\n |\\theta-c|\\leq\\frac{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)+2r}}b. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe distance from such a point to the interval in (1) is bounded by\n\\[\n \\left\\{\\frac{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)+2r}-\\sqrt{\\delta(P)}}b\\right\\}_+. \\tag{3}\n\\]\nIf \\(0\\leq\\delta(P)\\leq C_\\delta r\\), (3) is at most\n\\[\n \\frac{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)+2r}}b\n \\leq\\frac{\\sqrt{C_\\delta+2}}{c_\\beta}\\sqrt r. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\delta(P)\\geq\\delta_0\\), rationalizing (3) instead gives\n\\[\n \\frac{2r}{b\\{\\sqrt{\\delta(P)+2r}+\\sqrt{\\delta(P)}\\}}\n \\leq\\frac{2r}{c_\\beta\\sqrt{\\delta_0}}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over \\(\\theta\\in A_r\\) in (4) or (5) is exactly the outer directed-Hausdorff loss.  This proves the stated contact square-root modulus and interior linear modulus.",
    "justification": "The explicit modulus is the deterministic mechanism by which one root-\\(n\\) score procedure adapts continuously without regime selection.",
    "gap": "Generic criterion-set rate results do not isolate the exact square-root-to-linear modulus of this quadratic leakage score.",
    "consumer": "The adaptive score-hull theorem transfers its integrable score radius through this formula to obtain the continuous loss elbow.",
